# SlateCamp — Cursor Rules
# The second brain for board exam takers.
# Architecture students first. Multi-profession by design.

## Stack (Do Not Suggest Alternatives)
- Next.js 15 (App Router) — NOT Pages Router
- TypeScript strict mode — NO `any` types
- Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage + Edge Functions)
- `@supabase/ssr` for auth — NEVER localStorage
- Tailwind CSS 4 + shadcn/ui
- React Three Fiber + @react-three/drei for 3D
- Lucide React for icons (stroke 1.5px, 20px default)
- Stripe for payments
- PostHog for analytics
- Resend for email

## Architecture Rules
1. Server Components by default. Only add `'use client'` for useState, useEffect, event handlers, browser APIs.
2. Server Actions (`'use server'`) for ALL database mutations. Never expose Supabase writes client-side.
3. No `useEffect` for data fetching. Use RSC async functions or `use()` hook.
4. RLS is enabled on ALL tables. Check DATABASE_SCHEMA.sql before adding features.
5. TypeScript strict mode. Use Supabase-generated types from `types/supabase.ts`.
6. Mobile-first responsive. Touch targets minimum 44px. Bottom nav on mobile.
7. `useOptimistic` for quiz answer submissions. Sub-100ms perceived latency.

## Database
- The SINGLE source of truth is `DATABASE_SCHEMA.sql` at the project root.
- Do NOT create columns, tables, or types not defined there.
- Every table has RLS. Verify policies before shipping any feature.
- Exam Profiles scope EVERYTHING: sessions, analytics, SRS, 3D.

## Design System — "Rams Standard" (Dieter Rams / Braun inspired)
- Philosophy: "Less, but better." UI disappears so content breathes.
- Colors: Achromatic foundation (warm whites #F5F3F0, greys, charcoal #372E3D) with functional accents.
  - `--amber: #1f3055` → Primary CTA, progress, active states
  - `--green: #4A7C59` → Correct answer, success
  - `--red: #BF1B1B` → Incorrect answer, error
  - `--blue: #6B8FA3` → Info, links, secondary actions
  - `--gold: #BF7C2A` → Achievements, streaks
- NO gradients. NO heavy shadows (max: `0 1px 3px rgba(26,26,26,0.06)`).
- Transitions: All interactive elements `transition: all 150ms ease`. Mechanical, precise.
- Buttons: Solid fill for primary. Ghost/outline for secondary.
- Cards: White bg, 1px border #E8E4DF, border-radius 8px.
- Loading: Skeleton screens. NEVER spinners.
- Empty states: One line muted text + CTA button. No sad-face illustrations.

## The Three Modes
1. **Review Mode** — Doomscroll study. No analytics shown to user. Rich post-answer content.
2. **Sessions Mode** — Tracked, analytics-driven. Pre-built subject sessions OR custom compiled.
3. **3D Library** — Visual learning. Interactive models with callouts → quizzes.

## Quiz UI (THE MOST IMPORTANT SCREEN)
- Single column, `max-width: 720px`, centered
- Full-screen immersive on mobile — NOTHING else on screen
- Answer choices: full-width cards, min height 48px, 12px gap
- After answering: explanation + references + fun facts + related topics + 3D link
- For Sessions: show "Seen Nx, correct Yx (Z%)" per question (Pro/Max)
- Keyboard: 1-4 for answers, Enter for next. Swipe right on mobile.

## Question Schema
Every question has rich learning content: explanation, explanation_image_url, references (array with title/url/page/type), fun_facts (array), mnemonics, related_topic_ids, image_urls. See DATABASE_SCHEMA.sql `questions` table.

## Import Paths
- UI components: `@/components/ui/`
- Feature components: `@/components/{feature}/` (quiz, analytics, 3d, dashboard)
- Shared: `@/components/shared/`
- Lib: `@/lib/{module}/` (supabase, ai, srs, stripe, utils)
- Hooks: `@/hooks/`
- Types: `@/types/`

## Anti-patterns (NEVER DO THESE)
- Never use localStorage for auth state
- Never use useEffect for data fetching
- Never hardcode hex colors — always use CSS variables
- Never use `any` type — fix the actual type issue
- Never create tables/columns not in DATABASE_SCHEMA.sql
- Never show analytics data in Review Mode
- Never use spinner loading states — use skeletons
- Never use bouncy/spring animations — keep it Braun-mechanical
- Never use `console.log` in production — use proper error handling
